How the ACME HTTP-01 Validation Challenge Works
The ACME Protocol automates domain ownership validation without manual email or DNS verification:


Challenge Trigger: Certbot requests an SSL certificate for app.yourdomain.com from the Let's Encrypt ACME server.


Token Provisioning: Let's Encrypt responds with a cryptographic challenge token.


Local File Placement: Certbot places the token file in the Nginx web directory at /.well-known/acme-challenge/<TOKEN>.


Public Verification: Let's Encrypt sends an HTTP query to http://app.yourdomain.com/.well-known/acme-challenge/<TOKEN>.


Certificate Signing: Once the ACME server reads the token, domain control is proven, and signed TLS keys are delivered to the host.


# Key Takeaways You Will Gain:

DNS Management: Mapping public domain names to cloud IPs via A records and verifying propagation using diagnostic tools (dig, nslookup).

TLS/SSL Termination: Configuring Nginx to decrypt incoming HTTPS requests at the edge, relieving backend Node.js apps from handling heavy cryptographic handshakes.


Automated Certificate Lifecycle: Deploying certbot to automatically issue, configure, and renew Let's Encrypt certificates.


Security Enforcement: Applying HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) and automatic 301 Moved Permanently redirects from HTTP (80) to HTTPS (443).
